+- **0010** (LAN, on unless `BANANADS_LAN_GRACE_MS=0`): a missing MP reply is waited for, and its CMD sent again,
+  instead of reported. Real hardware sends a CMD again while a client's reply is missing; melonDS has that retry
+  off and tells the game at once that the client failed, so every late or lost packet reaches the game. Most games
+  shrug it off; Mario Party DS sometimes ends up in a state its link never leaves (40-byte CMDs, the same reply
+  over and over, the picture stopped while both sides keep talking: 2026-11-21 and -22, one or two lost exchanges
+  after a radio gap of ~60 ms, with dozens of earlier losses survived). Now the host waits up to the grace (300 ms
+  by default, `BANANADS_LAN_GRACE_MS`, 0-2000) and sends the CMD again every `BANANADS_LAN_RESEND_MS` (20); a
+  client that gets a CMD it already took (same timestamp) sends its reply to it again and doesn't hand it to the
+  DS twice. The ACK that ends an exchange goes out once more before the next CMD (and with each CMD sent
+  again), and a client takes each ACK once: a lost ACK left the client's game sure its reply hadn't arrived while
+  the host's was sure it had (2026-11-22: the same freeze with no failure reported at all). A client also waits out the grace (plus 100 ms) before 0014's step past a quiet host. The grace only
+  for players that replied lately (fewer than 3 CMDs in a row missed), so a console that left costs one grace,
+  then the usual timeout. A radio gap is a stall, not a lost exchange. `BANANADS_LAN_DROP` (0-500 per mille, test
+  only) drops that share of the MP frames sent; `BANANADS_LAN_DELAY_MS` (0-200, test only) delays them like a weak
+  radio (half-second stretches, `BANANADS_LAN_DELAY_BAD` per cent of them bad, default 10: each frame 0 to that
+  many ms late; order kept, nothing lost). "[diag] LAN recovery:" once a second when any of it happened.
 - Their other "[diag]" traces log at the Debug level only (`log.level = 0`).
